from common import lesson, lab, cite, out

LESSONS = [
lesson(10,3,'eigenvectors-and-covariance','Eigenvectors, eigenvalues, and the covariance matrix',
 'A symmetric matrix has a set of orthonormal directions it merely stretches. The covariance matrix of an activation batch is symmetric, and its eigenvectors are the directions along which the tokens spread most.',
 '''<h2 id="eigen">Directions a map only stretches</h2>
<p>Apply a square matrix A to many vectors and most of them change direction. An <strong>eigenvector</strong> v is one that does not: A v = λ v, the output is the input scaled by the <strong>eigenvalue</strong> λ. The 3Blue1Brown chapter on eigenvectors shows a shear leaving one line fixed; that line is the eigenvector direction. For a general matrix eigenvalues can be complex and eigenvectors need not be orthogonal, and this course avoids that case entirely, because the matrices whose spectra interpretability cares about are <strong>symmetric</strong>, A = Aᵀ, and for those everything is clean: the eigenvalues are real, and the eigenvectors can be chosen orthonormal, a full basis of the space in which A is diagonal.</p>
<div class="math">A v = λ v   (eigenvector v, eigenvalue λ)<br>for symmetric A:  A = V Λ Vᵀ,  V orthonormal, Λ diagonal<br>x · (A x) = Σᵢ λᵢ (x · vᵢ)²   for a unit vector x</div>
<p>The last line is the reading that matters. Write x in the eigenbasis as coordinates cᵢ = x · vᵢ; then the quadratic form x · A x is Σ λᵢ cᵢ². A symmetric matrix is a weighted sum of squared projections, with the eigenvalues as weights and the eigenvectors as the directions projected onto. The largest eigenvalue is the largest value of x · A x over unit vectors, attained at its eigenvector. Everything in lessons 11 and 12 is an instance of this sentence.</p>
<h2 id="covariance">The covariance matrix</h2>
<p>For an activation matrix X of shape (n, d), subtract the mean row to get X_c and form C = X_cᵀ X_c / (n − 1), a (d, d) matrix. Entry (i, j) is the covariance between coordinates i and j across tokens; the diagonal holds the variances. C is symmetric because covariance is. And for a unit direction u, u · C u is the variance of the projection coefficients X_c u, which you can verify by expanding: u · X_cᵀ X_c u / (n − 1) = ‖X_c u‖² / (n − 1). So the eigen-reading applies: the top eigenvector of C is the direction along which the tokens have the largest variance, and its eigenvalue is that variance. The lab computes C from the 301 non-outlier tokens and checks each claim.</p>
<pre class="term"><code>python3 labs/linalg_lab.py covariance</code></pre>
'''+out('''covariance of X (301, 768): shape (768, 768); symmetric: True
eigenvalues (eigh, reordered largest first), top five: [289.399 219.702 208.869 160.376 125.692]
smallest five: [-0. -0. -0. -0. -0.]  (n - 1 = 300 < 768, so at least 768 - 300 are zero up to rounding)
eigenvectors orthonormal: max |V.T V - I| = 2.89e-15
C v = lambda v for the top eigenvector: max |C v - lambda v| = 1.12e-13
sum of eigenvalues 3822.7 = total variance = sum of per-dimension variances 3822.7
singular values^2 / (n-1), top three: [289.399 219.702 208.869]  (same numbers as the eigenvalues)
variance along the top eigenvector 289.4; along neuron 447 55.7; along neuron 0 5.71''')+'''
<p>The top eigenvalue, 289.4, is the variance of the tokens along the top eigenvector, and the last line confirms it directly by projecting and taking the variance. Along neuron 447, the large coordinate from lesson 00, the variance is only 55.7, and along neuron 0 it is 5.71. The direction of greatest spread is not a neuron. The sum of all eigenvalues equals the sum of the 768 per-coordinate variances, 3,822.7, because the trace of a matrix is basis-independent; that total is what “variance explained” in lesson 11 is a fraction of. At least 468 eigenvalues are zero because 301 centred rows span at most 300 dimensions: the covariance of a small sample is always low-rank, and the zeros are a statement about the sample size, not the model.</p>
<p>The penultimate line previews lesson 12: the singular values of X_c, squared and divided by n − 1, are the eigenvalues of C. The SVD of the data matrix and the eigendecomposition of its covariance are the same computation, and in practice one uses the SVD because it avoids forming the (768, 768) product and squaring the rounding error.</p>
<h2 id="eigh">Computing it</h2>
<p><code>numpy.linalg.eigh</code> is the routine for symmetric matrices. It returns eigenvalues in ascending order, so the largest is last, and eigenvectors as columns of the second output. '''+cite('np-eigh')+''' It reads only one triangle of the input and never checks symmetry, which means that handing it a non-symmetric matrix silently decomposes a different matrix; the general routine <code>eig</code> exists for the non-symmetric case and returns complex numbers when it must. For covariance matrices and for the products AᵀA that lesson 12 uses, <code>eigh</code> is the right call.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Any time a (d, d) symmetric matrix appears, its eigenvectors are a basis in which it is a list of weights, and the top few eigenvectors are where its action is concentrated. Covariance, Gram matrices of feature directions, and the symmetric part of a bilinear form such as W_QK are the common cases.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Reading the top eigenvector of the covariance as “the most important direction for the model”. It is the direction of largest variance in this batch of tokens, which depends on the text, the tokens included, and whether position 0 was excluded. Lesson 11 shows the first component flipping meaning entirely when one row is added back.</p></div>''',
 'The covariance eigenvalues sum to 3,822.7 and the top one is 289.4. Someone reports “the first component explains 7.6% of the variance”. Someone else, using all 302 tokens including position 0, reports 88.9%. Both are correct computations. What does the disagreement tell you?',
 'That the total variance and its top direction are properties of the sample, and that one token with norm 3,047 contributes a squared deviation from the mean of order 3,000² ≈ 9 million against about 300 × 3,800 ≈ 1.1 million from all the other tokens together. With token 0 included the top eigenvector points from the mean toward token 0 and explains almost everything; without it the spectrum is spread across many directions. Neither number is wrong; neither is a fact about GPT-2 in general. Report which tokens went in.',
 lab(['python3 labs/linalg_lab.py covariance'],
 'Check by hand that the three largest singular values squared, divided by 300, reproduce the three largest eigenvalues. Then estimate how many eigenvalues you would need to add up to reach half the total variance, using lesson 11’s cumulative output.',
 'The singular values printed in lesson 12 are 294.652, 256.731, 250.321; squaring and dividing by 300 gives 289.4, 219.7, 208.9, the eigenvalues above. Lesson 11’s cumulative line shows 50% is reached between 10 and 20 components (38.4% at 10, 51.8% at 20).'), ['np-eigh','3b1b']),

lesson(11,3,'pca-on-activations','PCA on an activation matrix',
 'Principal component analysis finds the orthonormal directions of greatest variance and reports how much each explains. On the fixture it finds position; what it cannot find is anything you did not put in the batch.',
 '''<h2 id="what-pca-is">What the components are</h2>
<p><strong>PCA</strong> takes a centred data matrix X_c of shape (n, d) and returns orthonormal directions v₁, v₂, … such that v₁ is the unit direction of largest variance, v₂ the unit direction of largest variance among those orthogonal to v₁, and so on. By lesson 10 these are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix in decreasing eigenvalue order, and in practice they are the right singular vectors of X_c (lesson 12). The score of a token on component i is its projection coefficient (x − x̄) · vᵢ, so PCA is lesson 03 applied with directions that the data chose. <strong>Variance explained</strong> by component i is its eigenvalue λᵢ divided by Σ λⱼ, the fraction of the total variance lying along it; the cumulative sum tells you how much of the data’s spread k components capture.</p>
<div class="math">v₁ = argmax over unit u of Var(X_c u);  v₂ = the same with u ⟂ v₁; …<br>explained(i) = λᵢ / Σⱼ λⱼ<br>scores = X_c Vₖᵀ, shape (n, k);  rank-k reconstruction = scores Vₖ + x̄</div>
<h2 id="fixture">On the fixture, position 0 excluded</h2>
<pre class="term"><code>python3 labs/linalg_lab.py pca</code></pre>
'''+out('''PCA on tokens 1..301 (position 0 excluded): 10 components
variance explained: 0.076 0.057 0.055 0.042 0.033 0.030 0.027 0.024 0.021 0.019
cumulative:         0.076 0.133 0.188 0.230 0.263 0.293 0.320 0.345 0.365 0.384
  first   1 components:   7.6% of variance
  first   2 components:  13.3% of variance
  first   5 components:  26.3% of variance
  first  10 components:  38.4% of variance
  first  20 components:  51.8% of variance
  first  50 components:  72.6% of variance
  first 100 components:  88.1% of variance
components orthonormal: max |V V.T - I| = 3.11e-15
component 1, largest-magnitude neuron index 87 with weight -0.809
tokens at the two ends of component 1:
  low: ' tide' (-28), ' cargo' (-27), ' crossing' (-27), ' late' (-27), ' calm' (-27)
  high: '02' (27), ' households' (28), ' metres' (28), ' 2' (28), ' metres' (29)
  correlation of component-1 score with position: -0.923; component 2: 0.123
  correlation of component-1 score with is_digit: 0.200; component 2: 0.528
  correlation of component-1 score with after_period: 0.016; component 2: -0.098''')+'''
<h2 id="reading">How to read it</h2>
<p><em>The scree.</em> The variance-explained numbers 0.076, 0.057, 0.055, 0.042, … fall slowly. There is no elbow: no small number of components after which the rest are negligible. Ten components give 38%, fifty give 73%, a hundred give 88%. A <strong>scree plot</strong> of these numbers would be a gentle slope, and the honest summary is that the variation among these 301 tokens is spread across many directions. Had the first component explained 60% and the second 3%, the matrix would be nearly rank one in its variation, and one direction would describe the batch. It does not.</p>
<p><em>The first component.</em> Its largest neuron weight is −0.809 at index 87, so it is mostly but not only one coordinate. The tokens at its two ends are telling: the low end is the last sentence of the passage (tide, cargo, crossing, calm), the high end comes from the first two paragraphs (households, metres). Its score correlates with token position at −0.923. In this batch the direction of greatest variance is, to a first approximation, how far into the text the token is. That is a fact about this fixture that you could not have known before looking, and the right response is to ask whether it survives a different passage, not to announce that GPT-2 has a position direction.</p>
<p><em>The other labels.</em> Component 1 has correlation only 0.200 with the digit label; component 2 has 0.528. The digit signal that a probe reads with held-out correlation 0.986 is not concentrated in the top component and is only partly in the second. Variance and label-relevance are different criteria, and PCA optimises the first. Lesson 15 makes the comparison exact: the probe direction w has cosine 0.055 with component 1.</p>
<h2 id="not">What PCA does not tell you</h2>
<p>It does not tell you what the components mean. A component is a direction of spread, labelled afterwards by looking at which tokens score high and low, and the labelling is your inference, not the algorithm’s output. It does not find directions relevant to a label you have in mind unless that label happens to drive variance. It is not invariant to the choice of tokens: run <code>pca --keep-first</code> and the first component explains 88.9% of the variance and points at token 0, because that single row’s squared distance from the mean dwarfs everything else (lesson 10’s check question). It is not invariant to scaling of coordinates: if you standardise each of the 768 coordinates to unit variance you get different components, and neither choice is canonical. And a component explaining 7.6% of variance is not “unimportant”: the digit direction explains far less and predicts its label almost perfectly.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>PCA is a first look at a batch of activations and a baseline for any claimed direction: if your feature direction is just the top principal component, you have found the dominant variation in your prompt set, which may be position, sentence length, or a formatting artefact. Report the variance explained, the tokens that went in, and whether outliers were excluded.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Running PCA on uncentred activations. Without subtracting the mean, the first “component” is the direction of the mean vector itself, which in this layer is large (lesson 02), and the variance-explained numbers describe the mean rather than the spread.</p></div>''',
 'Component 1 correlates with position at −0.923. Would you report “GPT-2 layer 6 encodes position along its top principal component”?',
 'Not from this. The batch is one passage of 301 tokens, and anything that varies smoothly through a passage, such as topic, sentence structure or the running context, is confounded with position. The prediction test is the way to find out: before looking, state that the same component direction (not a refitted one) will correlate with position on a different passage, choose the correlation you would call a success, and run it. If it does, the claim is about the direction; if the correlation only reappears after refitting PCA on the new passage, it is about the method.',
 lab(['python3 labs/linalg_lab.py pca --keep-first --k 5'],
 'Include token 0 and rerun with five components. Read the variance explained and the two ends of component 1.',
 'The first component explains 88.9% of the variance and its score is extreme for one token, position 0, with everything else bunched near the other end. One outlier row has become the whole first component. This is the practical reason the other commands exclude position 0, and the general reason to compute norms before PCA.'), ['np-svd','np-eigh','arena']),

lesson(12,3,'svd-and-low-rank','SVD and low-rank structure',
 'Every matrix is a rotation, a stretch along orthogonal axes, and another rotation. The singular values are the stretch factors, and keeping the largest few is the best low-rank approximation there is.',
 '''<h2 id="definition">U Σ Vᵀ</h2>
<p>The <strong>singular value decomposition</strong> writes any matrix A of shape (n, d) as A = U Σ Vᵀ, where V has orthonormal columns in the input space ℝᵈ, U has orthonormal columns in the output space ℝⁿ, and Σ is diagonal with non-negative entries σ₁ ≥ σ₂ ≥ … called <strong>singular values</strong>. Reading it as a map applied to a row vector x: Vᵀ measures x along the input directions (coordinates in an orthonormal basis, lesson 06), Σ scales each coordinate by σᵢ, U places the result along the output directions. Nothing is lost and nothing mixes: input direction vᵢ goes to σᵢ times output direction uᵢ. The browser <a href="../appendices/b-explorers.html">SVD explorer</a> draws this for a 2×2 matrix as the image of the unit circle, an ellipse whose semi-axes are σ₁ u₁ and σ₂ u₂.</p>
<div class="math">A = U Σ Vᵀ = Σᵢ σᵢ uᵢ vᵢᵀ   (a sum of rank-one matrices)<br>A vᵢ = σᵢ uᵢ;  AᵀA vᵢ = σᵢ² vᵢ;  rank(A) = number of nonzero σᵢ<br>best rank-k approximation: Aₖ = Σᵢ≤ₖ σᵢ uᵢ vᵢᵀ,  with ‖A − Aₖ‖² = Σᵢ&gt;ₖ σᵢ²</div>
<p>Three consequences follow from the formula box. The singular values squared are the eigenvalues of the symmetric matrix AᵀA, so the SVD is the eigendecomposition of lesson 10 for a matrix that need not be square or symmetric. The rank is the number of nonzero singular values, which is how lesson 07 counted it. And truncating the sum after k terms gives the closest rank-k matrix to A in the sum-of-squares sense, with the error accounted for exactly by the dropped singular values; this is the Eckart–Young result, and the lab verifies the accounting numerically rather than proving it.</p>
<h2 id="weight-product">The SVD of a real weight product</h2>
<p>Lesson 07 found W_QK = W_Q W_Kᵀ to have rank 64. The SVD says more.</p>
<pre class="term"><code>python3 labs/linalg_lab.py svd</code></pre>
'''+out('''SVD of W_QK (768, 768): U (768, 768), S (768,), Vt (768, 768)
singular values: top five [21.014 19.039 18.155 17.238 16.563]; number above 1e-3 * max: 64; S[64:69] = [0. 0. 0. 0. 0.]
U @ diag(S) @ Vt reproduces W_QK: max error 3.43e-14
  rank- 1 approximation: relative error |W_QK - A_k| / |W_QK| = 0.9742
  rank- 8 approximation: relative error |W_QK - A_k| / |W_QK| = 0.8493
  rank-32 approximation: relative error |W_QK - A_k| / |W_QK| = 0.5401
  rank-64 approximation: relative error |W_QK - A_k| / |W_QK| = 0.0000
SVD of centred activations (301, 768): 301 singular values; top three [294.652 256.731 250.321]
  rank-  1: relative reconstruction error 0.9614; equals sqrt(sum of dropped S^2 / sum S^2) = 0.9614
  rank- 10: relative reconstruction error 0.7846; equals sqrt(sum of dropped S^2 / sum S^2) = 0.7846
  rank- 50: relative reconstruction error 0.5230; equals sqrt(sum of dropped S^2 / sum S^2) = 0.5230
  rank-100: relative reconstruction error 0.3445; equals sqrt(sum of dropped S^2 / sum S^2) = 0.3445
  rank-301: relative reconstruction error 0.0000; equals sqrt(sum of dropped S^2 / sum S^2) = 0.0000
right singular vectors of Xc are the PCA components: |cos| with pca component 1 = 1.000000''')+'''
<p>Exactly 64 singular values are nonzero; the 65th onward are zero to the printed precision. The (768, 768) matrix is a sum of 64 rank-one pieces σᵢ uᵢ vᵢᵀ, and the rank-64 approximation reproduces it with zero error because there is nothing beyond 64 to drop. Within the 64, the spectrum is flat: the top value is 21.0 and the eighth is still above 16, so the rank-8 truncation leaves 85% of the matrix behind. This head’s query-key map uses its 64 dimensions fairly evenly; a head with one dominant singular value would be nearly a rank-one bilinear form, reading one input direction against one other. Which kind a head is, you now know how to find out.</p>
<p>What the directions mean is stated as geometry: the right singular vectors vᵢ of W_QK are the directions in the residual stream of the query token that the map responds to, the left singular vectors uᵢ are the directions in the key token’s stream they are compared against, and σᵢ is how strongly. The framework paper describes the SVD of W_OV in the same terms, right singular vectors for the subspace “read in” and left singular vectors for the subspace written to. '''+cite('framework')+''' That is why the paper treats the low-rank products, rather than the four factor matrices, as the objects to study: the factors can be changed without changing the product, but the product’s SVD is a property of the head. The transformers course does the circuits; this lesson only shows that the SVD is the right tool for a map that is a product through a bottleneck.</p>
<h2 id="activations">The SVD of the activation matrix</h2>
<p>The second half of the output is the SVD of the centred activations, and its right singular vectors are the PCA components (cosine 1.000000 with component 1). The reconstruction-error lines are the Eckart–Young accounting on real data: the relative error of the rank-10 reconstruction is 0.7846, and the square root of the dropped singular values’ share of the total is also 0.7846, on every line. The error is not small: ten components reconstruct the tokens to within 78% relative error, consistent with the slow scree of lesson 11. At rank 301 nothing is dropped, because 301 centred rows have at most 301 nonzero singular values (in fact at most 300, since centring removes one), and the error is zero.</p>
<h2 id="code">Computing it</h2>
<p><code>numpy.linalg.svd</code> returns U, the singular values in descending order, and Vᵀ (not V); with <code>full_matrices=False</code> the shapes are (n, k), (k,), (k, d) with k = min(n, d), and <code>(U * S) @ Vt</code> reconstructs the matrix. '''+cite('np-svd')+''' Keep the descending order and the transposed V in mind: the first row of Vt is the top right singular vector.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Whenever a matrix is a product through a smaller dimension (head products, low-rank adapters, factorised embeddings), its SVD lists what it reads, what it writes, and how much, in rank order. Whenever a matrix is data, its SVD is PCA. The same decomposition, two readings; the singular value spectrum tells you in both cases whether a few directions carry the action.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Reading U and V from the wrong side. In the row-vector convention x ↦ x A, the input directions are the rows of Vᵀ and the output directions the columns of U; in the column convention A x they swap roles. Check with A vᵢ = σᵢ uᵢ on one vector before interpreting either.</p></div>''',
 'W_QK has 64 nonzero singular values with the top at 21.0 and the eighth above 16. If a different head had singular values 40, 2, 1, 0.5, … what could you say about it without any further information?',
 'That its action on the residual stream is nearly rank one: it responds to one input direction v₁ and compares it against one output direction u₁ with weight 40, and every other pairing is at least twenty times weaker. A rank-one bilinear form q · k = 40 (x_q · v₁)(x_k · u₁) is a single projection on each side multiplied together, and the way to interpret the head would be to project tokens onto v₁ and u₁ and read the scalars, lesson 03 twice. The flat spectrum of the fixture head means no such shortcut exists for it.',
 lab(['python3 labs/linalg_lab.py svd'],
 'Square the top singular value of the centred activations, 294.652, and divide by n − 1 = 300. Compare with the top covariance eigenvalue from lesson 10. Then explain from the formula box why the rank-301 reconstruction error must be zero.',
 '294.652² / 300 = 289.4, the top eigenvalue of lesson 10: the two decompositions are one computation. The centred matrix has 301 rows, so it has at most 301 nonzero singular values (300 after centring); a rank-301 approximation keeps every term of the sum, drops nothing, and the dropped-share formula gives √0 = 0.'), ['np-svd','framework','3b1b']),
]
